Subject: [PATCH] cw.c: fix a coloring bug introduced by 1528c51d793

A declaration was brought TOO close to the point of use. Back it off!

diff --git a/cw.c b/cw.c
index f6cf1f0..b0ff6bc 100644
--- a/cw.c
+++ b/cw.c
@@ -408,6 +408,7 @@ noreturn void execcw(int argc,char **argv){
    int fdm=MAX(fds[0],fde[0])+1,fd=0,e=0;
    char *tmp=NULL;
    fd_set rfds;
+   ssize_t j=0;
    for(ssize_t s=0;s>0||!ext;){
     FD_ZERO(&rfds);
     FD_SET(fds[0],&rfds);
@@ -417,7 +418,6 @@ noreturn void execcw(int argc,char **argv){
      else if(FD_ISSET(fde[0],&rfds))fd=fde[0];
      else continue;
      memset(buf,0,BUFSIZE);
-     ssize_t j=0;
      while((s=read(fd,buf,BUFSIZE))>0){
       if(!tmp){
        j=0;
-- 
2.25.1

        You will be given the root cause of a bug, the commit message and the corresponding patch.
        You need to extract all code statements that lead to the bug from the patch, based on the root cause and the commit message.

        Root cause:
        The bug was caused by the declaration of the variable `j` being placed inside the while loop, which limited its scope to that loop. This meant that `j` would be re-initialized to 0 on each iteration, preventing it from retaining its value across iterations and leading to incorrect behavior in the program's logic.

        Commit message:
        cw.c: fix a coloring bug introduced by 1528c51d793

A declaration was brought TOO close to the point of use. Back it off!  

